) VALUES

('NCLEX_SATA_521041', 'SATA',
 'A 7-year-old is admitted with acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis. Which interventions should the nurse include in the plan of care? Select all that apply.',
 'During the acute phase the inflamed glomeruli retain sodium and water, so the child is at risk of hypertension, circulatory overload and encephalopathy. Daily weight on the same scales at the same time is the most sensitive measure of fluid retention, and regular blood pressure with accurate fluid balance detects deterioration early. Sodium is restricted while oedema and hypertension persist, and fluids are limited rather than encouraged.',
 '{"options":[{"id":"A","text":"Weigh the child daily on the same scales at the same time"},{"id":"B","text":"Record blood pressure every 4 hours"},{"id":"C","text":"Measure urine output and record the colour of each specimen"},{"id":"D","text":"Encourage a fluid intake of at least 3 litres per day"},{"id":"E","text":"Provide a diet with no added salt"},{"id":"F","text":"Encourage foods high in potassium such as bananas and orange juice"}]}'::jsonb,
 '{"answers":["A","B","C","E"],"feedback":{"A":"Correct. Daily weight is the most sensitive indicator of fluid retention or loss.","B":"Correct. Hypertension is the complication that can progress to encephalopathy and must be tracked.","C":"Correct. Volume and colour together show whether the glomerular inflammation is settling.","D":"Incorrect. Fluids are restricted, not encouraged, while output is reduced.","E":"Correct. Restricting sodium limits further water retention and helps control blood pressure.","F":"Incorrect. Potassium may rise when output falls, so high-potassium foods are avoided."}}'::jsonb,
 'Physiological Integrity', 'Basic Care and Comfort',
 'Pediatrics', 'Genitourinary', 'Acute glomerulonephritis', 'Acute phase monitoring',
 'Medium', 'CURATOR_LABEL', 'Apply', '{}', 'Select all that apply.', 4, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE,
 'gapfill-20260728'),

('NCLEX_MCQ_521042', 'MCQ',
 'A newborn is found to have hypospadias at the routine newborn examination. Which information should the nurse give the parents?',
 'In hypospadias the urethral opening lies on the underside of the penis rather than at the tip, and surgical repair is usually carried out between 6 and 12 months of age. The foreskin is frequently used as graft tissue during that repair, so circumcision is deferred until the surgeon has decided the plan. Explaining this early prevents a well-meaning but irreversible procedure being requested before discharge.',
 '{"options":[{"id":"A","text":"Circumcision will be deferred because the foreskin may be needed for the surgical repair"},{"id":"B","text":"Circumcision should be carried out before discharge to reduce the risk of infection"},{"id":"C","text":"Repair is performed only after the second birthday and only if symptoms develop"},{"id":"D","text":"Urine will need to be drained by catheter until the repair is carried out"}]}'::jsonb,
 '{"answer":"A","feedback":{"A":"Correct. The foreskin is often used as graft tissue, so circumcision is deferred until after surgical planning.","B":"Incorrect. Circumcision may remove tissue the surgeon needs and is therefore avoided.","C":"Incorrect. Repair is usually undertaken between 6 and 12 months, before body image and toilet training are affected.","D":"Incorrect. The newborn voids normally through the misplaced opening and does not need a catheter."}}'::jsonb,
 'Health Promotion and Maintenance', 'Health Promotion and Maintenance',
 'Pediatrics', 'Genitourinary', 'Hypospadias', 'Deferring circumcision',
 'Very easy', 'CURATOR_LABEL', 'Understand', '{}', NULL, 1, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE,
 'gapfill-20260728'),

('NCLEX_MCQ_521043', 'MCQ',
 'A 9-year-old with sickle cell disease is admitted with severe pain in both legs and the lower back. The temperature is 37.8 degrees Celsius and oxygen saturation is 96 per cent in air. Which nursing action is the PRIORITY?',
 'In a vaso-occlusive crisis, sickled cells block small vessels and cause ischaemic tissue pain, and the two interventions that shorten the crisis are rehydration to reduce blood viscosity and effective analgesia, usually with opioids on a regular schedule. Fear of dependence leads to persistent undertreatment of pain in this group, and untreated pain itself worsens sickling through stress and hypoxia. Cold causes vasoconstriction and increases sickling, so warmth rather than cold is applied.',
 '{"options":[{"id":"A","text":"Start the prescribed intravenous fluids and give the prescribed opioid analgesia"},{"id":"B","text":"Apply cold packs to the painful limbs"},{"id":"C","text":"Encourage vigorous active exercise of both legs"},{"id":"D","text":"Restrict oral fluids until the pain has settled"}]}'::jsonb,
 '{"answer":"A","feedback":{"A":"Correct. Hydration reduces blood viscosity and opioid analgesia treats genuinely severe ischaemic pain.","B":"Incorrect. Cold causes vasoconstriction, which increases sickling and worsens the crisis.","C":"Incorrect. Vigorous exercise raises oxygen demand and aggravates the crisis; gentle movement is allowed as pain permits.","D":"Incorrect. Restricting fluids concentrates the blood and promotes further sickling."}}'::jsonb,
 'Physiological Integrity', 'Basic Care and Comfort',
 'Pediatrics', 'Hematologic', 'Sickle cell disease', 'Vaso-occlusive crisis management',
 'Hard', 'CURATOR_LABEL', 'Apply', '{}', NULL, 1, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE,
 'gapfill-20260728'),

('NCLEX_SATA_521044', 'SATA',
 'A nurse is teaching the parents of a 4-year-old newly diagnosed with haemophilia A. Which instructions should be included? Select all that apply.',
 'Haemophilia is a deficiency of clotting factor, so home management aims to avoid trauma and to avoid anything that further impairs haemostasis. Aspirin and non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs inhibit platelet function and are replaced by paracetamol, and a soft toothbrush prevents gum bleeding. Non-contact activity is encouraged rather than inactivity, and first aid for a bleed is pressure, cold and elevation, followed by factor replacement.',
 '{"options":[{"id":"A","text":"Give paracetamol rather than aspirin or ibuprofen for pain"},{"id":"B","text":"Use a soft toothbrush and supervise daily dental care"},{"id":"C","text":"Encourage swimming and cycling with a helmet rather than contact sports"},{"id":"D","text":"Apply firm pressure and a cold pack to a bleeding site and elevate the limb"},{"id":"E","text":"Ask that all immunisations be given by the intramuscular route"},{"id":"F","text":"Massage a swollen joint vigorously to disperse the blood"}]}'::jsonb,
 '{"answers":["A","B","C","D"],"feedback":{"A":"Correct. Aspirin and ibuprofen impair platelet function and add to the bleeding risk.","B":"Correct. A soft brush prevents gum trauma, which is a common source of prolonged bleeding.","C":"Correct. Activity is encouraged for joint and muscle health, but contact sports are avoided.","D":"Correct. Pressure, cold and elevation limit bleeding until factor replacement is given.","E":"Incorrect. Subcutaneous administration with pressure afterwards is preferred to reduce deep muscle bleeding.","F":"Incorrect. Massage of a bleeding joint increases bleeding and pain; the joint is rested and elevated."}}'::jsonb,
 'Health Promotion and Maintenance', 'Health Promotion and Maintenance',
 'Pediatrics', 'Hematologic', 'Haemophilia', 'Home safety teaching',
 'Easy', 'CURATOR_LABEL', 'Apply', '{}', 'Select all that apply.', 4, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE,
 'gapfill-20260728'),

('NCLEX_MCQ_521045', 'MCQ',
 'A nurse is teaching the parent of a 15-month-old who has been prescribed liquid ferrous sulfate for iron deficiency anaemia. Which statement by the parent indicates correct understanding?',
 'Iron is absorbed best in an acid environment on a relatively empty stomach, and vitamin C enhances absorption, whereas milk, tea and antacids bind iron and reduce it substantially. Liquid iron stains developing teeth, so it is given through a straw or placed towards the back of the mouth with a dropper, and the mouth is rinsed afterwards. Black tarry stools are an expected and harmless effect, not a reason to stop.',
 '{"options":[{"id":"A","text":"I will give it between meals with orange juice, using a dropper towards the back of the mouth"},{"id":"B","text":"I will mix it into a full bottle of milk so it goes down more easily"},{"id":"C","text":"I will stop the medicine if the stools turn black"},{"id":"D","text":"I will give it with a cup of tea to settle the stomach"}]}'::jsonb,
 '{"answer":"A","feedback":{"A":"Correct. Between meals with vitamin C maximises absorption, and a dropper protects the teeth from staining.","B":"Incorrect. Milk binds iron and markedly reduces absorption.","C":"Incorrect. Black stools are an expected effect of oral iron and are not a reason to stop treatment.","D":"Incorrect. Tannins in tea bind iron and reduce absorption considerably."}}'::jsonb,
 'Physiological Integrity', 'Pharmacological and Parenteral Therapies',
 'Pediatrics', 'Hematologic', 'Iron deficiency anaemia', 'Oral iron administration',
 'Easy', 'CURATOR_LABEL', 'Apply', '{}', NULL, 1, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE,
 'gapfill-20260728'),

('NCLEX_SELN_521046', 'SELECT_N',
 'A 6-year-old receiving chemotherapy for acute lymphoblastic leukaemia has a neutrophil count of 0.4 x 10^9/L. Which 3 actions should the nurse take?',
 'Severe neutropenia removes the ability to mount an inflammatory response, so infection may present only as fever and can progress within hours. Protective measures reduce exposure: a single room, exclusion of visitors with infection, removal of fresh flowers, plants and unpeeled raw foods that carry bacteria and fungal spores. Rectal thermometers, suppositories and live vaccines are avoided because they either breach the mucosa or introduce live organisms.',
 '{"options":[{"id":"A","text":"Nurse the child in a single room and exclude visitors with any infection"},{"id":"B","text":"Measure the temperature by the axillary route rather than the rectal route"},{"id":"C","text":"Remove fresh flowers and uncooked salad from the room"},{"id":"D","text":"Encourage the child to join group play sessions in the ward playroom"},{"id":"E","text":"Administer the scheduled live varicella vaccine"},{"id":"F","text":"Bathe the child daily using scented bubble bath"}],"select_count":3}'::jsonb,
 '{"answers":["A","B","C"],"feedback":{"A":"Correct. Reducing exposure to other people is the core of protective isolation.","B":"Correct. Rectal measurement can breach fragile mucosa and introduce bacteraemia.","C":"Correct. Flowers, plants and raw produce carry bacteria and fungal spores.","D":"Incorrect. Group play exposes a severely neutropenic child to other children and their infections.","E":"Incorrect. Live vaccines are contraindicated during immunosuppression.","F":"Incorrect. Scented products irritate and dry the skin, which is an important barrier to infection."}}'::jsonb,
 'Safe and Effective Care Environment', 'Safety and Infection Control',
 'Pediatrics', 'Hematologic', 'Neutropenia in childhood leukaemia', 'Protective measures',
 'Medium', 'CURATOR_LABEL', 'Apply', '{}', 'Select 3.', 3, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE,
 'gapfill-20260728'),

('NCLEX_CLZ_521047', 'CLOZE',
 'A 14-year-old with type 1 diabetes becomes shaky, pale and sweaty during a physical education lesson, and the school nurse records a blood glucose of 3.1 mmol/L. The nurse should first give {1} and then recheck the blood glucose after {2}.',
 'Hypoglycaemia in a conscious adolescent is treated with about 15 g of a rapidly absorbed carbohydrate, such as glucose tablets or fruit juice, because fat and protein slow gastric emptying and delay the rise. The glucose is rechecked after 15 minutes and the dose repeated if it remains low, which is the rule of 15. Once the glucose is above 4 mmol/L, a longer-acting carbohydrate such as bread or a sandwich prevents a second fall.',
 '{"blanks":[{"id":"b1","choices":[{"id":"c1","text":"15 g of a fast-acting carbohydrate such as glucose tablets or fruit juice"},{"id":"c2","text":"the usual dose of rapid-acting insulin"},{"id":"c3","text":"a slice of wholemeal bread with peanut butter"}]},{"id":"b2","choices":[{"id":"c1","text":"5 minutes"},{"id":"c2","text":"15 minutes"},{"id":"c3","text":"60 minutes"}]}]}'::jsonb,
 '{"answers":{"b1":"c1","b2":"c2"},"feedback":{}}'::jsonb,
 'Physiological Integrity', 'Pharmacological and Parenteral Therapies',
 'Pediatrics', 'Endocrine', 'Type 1 diabetes in adolescence', 'Treating hypoglycaemia',
 'Medium', 'CURATOR_LABEL', 'Apply', '{}', 'Complete the sentence by choosing from each drop-down.', 2, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE,
 'gapfill-20260728'),

('NCLEX_MCQ_521048', 'MCQ',
 'A nurse is teaching the parents of a newborn diagnosed with congenital hypothyroidism through newborn screening, who has been prescribed levothyroxine. Which statement indicates that teaching has been effective?',
 'Thyroid hormone is essential for brain development in the first years of life, so treatment must start within the first weeks and continue without interruption to prevent irreversible intellectual disability. The tablet is crushed and mixed with a small amount of water or breast milk and given at the same time each day, and it is not given with soya formula, iron or calcium, which bind the drug and reduce absorption. A missed dose is given as soon as it is remembered; doses are never doubled.',
 '{"options":[{"id":"A","text":"I will crush the tablet, mix it with a small amount of water or breast milk, give it at the same time each morning, and continue it for life"},{"id":"B","text":"I will mix the tablet into a full bottle of soya formula"},{"id":"C","text":"We can stop the medicine once the baby is growing well"},{"id":"D","text":"I will give a double dose if a dose was missed the day before"}]}'::jsonb,
 '{"answer":"A","feedback":{"A":"Correct. Consistent daily dosing in a small volume of liquid, continued lifelong, is what protects brain development.","B":"Incorrect. Soya binds levothyroxine and significantly reduces absorption, and a full bottle may not be finished.","C":"Incorrect. Normal growth reflects successful treatment; stopping causes hypothyroidism to return.","D":"Incorrect. Doubling a dose risks thyrotoxic effects such as tachycardia and irritability."}}'::jsonb,
 'Physiological Integrity', 'Pharmacological and Parenteral Therapies',
 'Pediatrics', 'Endocrine', 'Congenital hypothyroidism', 'Levothyroxine administration',
 'Hard', 'CURATOR_LABEL', 'Apply', '{}', NULL, 1, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE,
 'gapfill-20260728'),

('NCLEX_MMR_521049', 'MATRIX_MR',
 'A nurse is admitting several children with communicable illnesses to a paediatric ward. For each illness, select all the transmission-based precautions required in addition to standard precautions.',
 'Transmission-based precautions follow the route the organism actually travels. Varicella needs both airborne and contact precautions because the virus is carried on air currents and is also present in the vesicle fluid, whereas measles is airborne only. Pertussis, mumps and group A streptococcal illness such as scarlet fever spread on large respiratory droplets that fall within a short distance, so droplet precautions apply until the child has had effective antibiotics or the infectious period has passed.',
 '{"rows":[{"id":"r1","text":"Chickenpox (varicella)"},{"id":"r2","text":"Measles"},{"id":"r3","text":"Pertussis (whooping cough)"},{"id":"r4","text":"Mumps"},{"id":"r5","text":"Scarlet fever (group A streptococcus)"}],"columns":[{"id":"c1","text":"Airborne"},{"id":"c2","text":"Droplet"},{"id":"c3","text":"Contact"}],"row_label":"Illness"}'::jsonb,
 '{"cells":{"r1":["c1","c3"],"r2":["c1"],"r3":["c2"],"r4":["c2"],"r5":["c2"]},"feedback":{}}'::jsonb,
 'Safe and Effective Care Environment', 'Safety and Infection Control',
 'Pediatrics', 'Immune', 'Communicable childhood illness', 'Transmission-based precautions',
 'Very hard', 'CURATOR_LABEL', 'Analyze', '{}', 'Select all that apply for each row.', 6, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE,
 'gapfill-20260728'),

('NCLEX_MCQ_521050', 'MCQ',
 'A 3-year-old is being discharged after treatment for Kawasaki disease with intravenous immunoglobulin and is continuing low-dose aspirin at home. Which instruction is most important for the nurse to give the parents?',
 'Kawasaki disease is one of the few paediatric indications for aspirin, given first in high dose for inflammation and then in low dose for its antiplatelet effect while the coronary arteries are at risk of aneurysm. Aspirin taken during a varicella or influenza infection is strongly associated with Reye syndrome, an acute encephalopathy with fatty liver, so exposure must be reported and the therapy reviewed. Parents are also told that live vaccines are deferred for about 11 months after immunoglobulin, because passive antibody blunts the response.',
 '{"options":[{"id":"A","text":"Contact the clinic if the child is exposed to chickenpox or influenza, because the aspirin must be reviewed to reduce the risk of Reye syndrome"},{"id":"B","text":"Give the aspirin only on days when the child has a fever"},{"id":"C","text":"Attend for the scheduled live vaccines during the next month as usual"},{"id":"D","text":"Stop the aspirin as soon as the rash and the peeling of the fingers have resolved"}]}'::jsonb,
 '{"answer":"A","feedback":{"A":"Correct. Aspirin during varicella or influenza is linked with Reye syndrome, so exposure must prompt urgent review.","B":"Incorrect. Low-dose aspirin is continued daily for its antiplatelet effect, not used as an antipyretic.","C":"Incorrect. Live vaccines are deferred for about 11 months after immunoglobulin because passive antibody blocks the response.","D":"Incorrect. Aspirin continues until coronary artery imaging confirms that no aneurysm has formed."}}'::jsonb,
 'Physiological Integrity', 'Pharmacological and Parenteral Therapies',
 'Pediatrics', 'Cardiovascular', 'Kawasaki disease', 'Aspirin therapy and Reye syndrome',
 'Hard', 'CURATOR_LABEL', 'Apply', '{}', NULL, 1, TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE,
 'gapfill-20260728');
